<tr><td style=3D"padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style=3D"font-family:Georgia,serif;=
font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">Markets spent most of Tuesday pretending th=
at nothing had happened, which in this cycle is its own kind of news. The ind=
ex closed up eleven points, a move so small it barely qualifies as an opinion=
, and yet the underlying plumbing told a much less calm story.</p></td></tr><=
tr><td style=3D"padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style=3D"font-family:Georgia,serif;f=
ont-size:16px;line-height:24px;">Underlying yields rose for the fourth straig=
ht session. That matters because everything in the last two years has been pr=
iced off the assumption that borrowing costs had peaked. Four straight up day=
s in the long bond is the market starting to doubt that assumption out loud.<=
/p></td></tr><tr><td style=3D"padding:0 32px 12px;"><p style=3D"font-family:G=
eorgia,serif;font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">The energy complex was the oth=
er tell. Crude sold off hard into the London close, then quietly recovered in=
 the American afternoon. Nobody at the exchanges will say why, and the volume=
 profile suggests a single large seller rather than a change in fundamentals.=
</p></td></tr><tr><td class=3D"footer" style=3D"padding:24px;font-size:12px;c=
